 "sections": {
  "The setup": "Sea surface temperatures of 29–30 °C, abundant moisture and a deep warm layer. JTWC expects the storm to move into ocean heat content of 120–150 kJ/cm² — roughly a layer of water warmer than 26 °C about 150 m deep (our conversion). Around 50 kJ/cm² is enough to support intensification; 120+ means a storm can churn the ocean without bringing up cool water. During El Niño, typhoon genesis shifts south-east toward the date line, so storms form farther east and spend longer over warm open ocean before reaching land. That is how Guam has been threatened three times since April.",
  "The trigger": "A disturbance in the trade-wind easterlies (Invest 96W) consolidated into Tropical Depression 29W early on 15 October and was named Champi later that day.",
  "What amplified it": "Strong outflow toward the west (the storm could vent its rising air easily aloft), warm deep water and plenty of moisture. Once an inner core formed it intensified by 40 kt in 23 hours (35 kt to 75 kt), above the 30-kt-in-24-hours threshold for rapid intensification. JTWC expects rapid intensification to continue for about 48 hours; one model (HAFS-A) shows a peak as high as 160 kt.",
  "What held it back": "Moderate vertical wind shear early on (15–20 kt, about 17–23 mph) slowed organisation on 15–16 October. Shear is forecast to fall below 10 kt by 18 October.",
  "What steers it": "The subtropical high to the north is steering Champi west to west-northwest at about 15 mph. Model tracks range from just south of Guam to the Guam–Rota channel, so NWS Guam told residents not to focus on the exact track. Beyond 20 October the storm should round the western edge of the high; whether it then turns north toward the Ryukyus depends on a trough over East Asia.",
  "Expected impacts": "Guam (NWS, 17 Oct): winds 105–120 mph with gusts to 155 mph, 12–20 in of rain, surf up to 35 ft and coastal inundation of 5–7 ft. Rota: 60–80 mph, gusts to 115 mph. Saipan and Tinian: 40–60 mph. Five school evacuation centres open from 10 am Saturday; the Red Cross is opening 13 shelters; families displaced by Sinlaku and Bavi must take down tents and move to shelters.",
  "How it should end": "Not before 21–23 October: it is forecast to stay a very strong typhoon over the Philippine Sea. Its end depends on recurvature and colder water to the north.",
  "Climate signal": "No study. El Niño shifts typhoon tracks east and lengthens their lives; this is a well-documented ENSO effect, not attribution.",
  "Still open": "Closest approach, peak winds on Guam, damage, and the track after the Philippine Sea.",
  "What happened so far": "Guam: closest approach within 12 miles of the southern tip on 18 October; damage to homes and trees, shelters for more than 1,000 people, power and water outages (up to 170 wells off); southern areas not yet assessed as of 22 October. No deaths reported. Taiwan, Ryukyus, eastern China, Korea and Japan: forecast impacts as listed in the timeline; to be scored after the storm."
 },
